<http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/VolumetricHeatCapacity> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "$\\textit{Volumetric Heat Capacity (VHC)}$, also termed $\\textit{volume-specific heat capacity}$, describes the ability of a given volume of a substance to store internal energy while undergoing a given temperature change, but without undergoing a phase transition. It is different from specific heat capacity in that the VHC is a $\\textit{per unit volume}$ measure of the relationship between thermal energy and temperature of a material, while the specific heat is a $\\textit{per unit mass}$ measure (or occasionally per molar quantity of the material)." .
