<http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/StandardGravitationalParameter> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "In celestial mechanics the standard gravitational parameter of a celestial body is the product of the gravitational constant G and the mass M of the body. Expressed as $\\mu = G \\cdot M$. The SI units of the standard gravitational parameter are $m^{3}s^{-2}$." .
<http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/AtmosphericPressure> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "The pressure exerted by the weight of the air above it at any point on the earth's surface. At sea level the atmosphere will support a column of mercury about $760 mm$ high. This decreases with increasing altitude. The standard value for the atmospheric pressure at sea level in SI units is $101,325 pascals$." .
