<http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/EnergyExpenditure> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "Energy expenditure is dependent on a person's sex, metabolic rate, body-mass composition, the thermic effects of food, and activity level.  The approximate energy expenditure of a man lying in bed is $1.0\\,kilo\\,calorie\\,per\\,hour\\,per\\,kilogram$. For slow walking (just over two miles per hour), $3.0\\,kilo\\,calorie\\,per\\,hour\\,per\\,kilogram$. For fast steady running (about 10 miles per hour), $16.3\\,kilo\\,calorie\\,per\\,hour\\,per\\,kilogram$. Females expend about 10 per cent less energy than males of the same size doing a comparable activity.  For people weighing the same, individuals with a high percentage of body fat usually expend less energy than lean people, because fat is not as metabolically active as muscle." .
