<http://www.loa-cnr.it/ontologies/DOLCE-Lite.owl#achievement> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Eventive occurrences (events) are called achievements  if they are atomic, otherwise they are accomplishments.Further developments: being 'achievement', 'accomplishment', 'state', 'event', etc. can be also considered 'aspects' of processes or of parts of them. For example, the same process 'rock erosion in the Sinni valley' can be seen as an accomplishment (what has brought the current state that e.g. we are trying to explain), as an achievement (the erosion process as the result of a previous accomplishment), as a state (collapsing the time interval of the erosion into a time point), as an event (what has changed our focus from a state to another).In the erosion case, we could have good motivations to shift from one aspect to another: a) causation focus, b) effectual focus, c) condensation d) transition (causality)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
