<http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/ElectromotiveForce> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> " In physics, $\\textit{Electromotive Force}$, or most commonly $emf$ (seldom capitalized), or (occasionally) electromotance is that which tends to cause current (actual electrons and ions) to flow. More formally, $emf$ is the external work expended per unit of charge to produce an electric potential difference across two open-circuited terminals. $\\textit{Electromotive Force}$ is deprecated in the ISO System of Quantities. " .
