<http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/HeatCapacityRatio> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "The heat capacity ratio, or ratio of specific heats, is the ratio of the heat capacity at constant pressure ($C_P$) to heat capacity at constant volume ($C_V$). For an ideal gas, the heat capacity is constant with temperature ($\\theta$). Accordingly we can express the enthalpy as $H = C_P*\\theta$ and the internal energy as $U = C_V \\cdot \\theta$. Thus, it can also be said that the heat capacity ratio is the ratio between enthalpy and internal energy." .
